.TH FASTVM 1 "March 2026" "fastvm 1.0.0" "FastVM Manual"
.SH NAME
fastvm \- rapid VM provisioning for parallel AI agent workloads
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fastvm
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fastvm
provisions isolated VMs through the configured FastVM API backend in seconds.
It is designed for AI agents that need to spin up parallel compute environments,
run experiments, and converge on a winner.
.PP
The CLI runs on your local machine and talks to the
.B fastvm-api
server running on the backend host. All VM management happens through the API.
.SS THE PARADIGM
AI agents work best when they can iterate in isolation. fastvm enables this:
.PP
.RS 4
.B 1. CREATE
\(em Spin up a base VM from a stock template.
.br
.B 2. SET UP
\(em SSH in and install your application, tools, and configuration.
.br
.B 3. SNAPSHOT
\(em Convert the configured VM to a reusable template with
.BR "fastvm snapshot" .
.br
.B 4. DIVERGE
\(em Clone N copies from your custom template.
.br
.B 5. EXPERIMENT
\(em Each agent works in its own VM. Build, break things freely.
.br
.B 6. BENCHMARK
\(em Measure and compare results across VMs.
.br
.B 7. CONVERGE
\(em Pick the winner with
.B fastvm converge
and destroy the rest.
.RE
.PP
The snapshot step turns a one-off VM into a reusable starting point.
Instead of cloning from generic OS templates, clone from your own pre-configured image.
This is the core loop: create, snapshot, diverge, experiment, converge.
Repeat at any level of granularity.
.PP
Each VM gets its own static IP, internet access, and internal DNS.
VMs are network-isolated per user but can reach the internet and internal
services (*.query.prod.telnyx.io, *.query.dev.telnyx.io).
.SS SSH ACCESS
VMs are accessible via SSH proxy \(em no hypervisor access needed.
Each VM's SSH port equals its VMID (e.g., VMID 60000 listens on port 60000).
A
.B socat
process on the backend host forwards connections from the public port to
the VM's internal SSH port (22).
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
ssh \-p 60000 ubuntu@backend\-host    \(->    VM:22
.fi
.RE
.PP
Users never see internal IPs. They just need the backend host address and the
port number (which equals the VMID). The
.B ssh_command
field in the API response and CLI output provides the complete command.
.PP
The proxy starts automatically when a VM is created and stops when the VM
is destroyed. Orphan proxies from previous runs are cleaned up on API startup.
.PP
Requirements:
.B socat
must be installed on the backend host. Ports 60000\-60999 must be open
in any firewall.
.SS FOR AI AGENTS
If you are an AI agent using fastvm, here is how to work with it:
.PP
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Call
.B fastvm create
to get VMs. The response includes SSH commands with port\-forwarded access.
.IP \(bu 2
SSH into your VM using the
.B ssh_command
from the response (e.g.,
.BR "ssh \-p 60000 ubuntu@host" ).
You have sudo. Install what you need. The VM has internet.
.IP \(bu 2
The SSH port equals the VMID. No hypervisor access or internal IPs needed.
.IP \(bu 2
Internal DNS resolves Telnyx services at 10.255.0.100.
.IP \(bu 2
When done, call
.B fastvm converge --keep <your-vmid>
or
.B fastvm destroy
to clean up.
.IP \(bu 2
VMs are ephemeral. Do not store persistent data. Extract results before destroying.
.RE
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B create \-u USER \-c COUNT [\-m MEM] [\-\-cores N] [\-t TEMPLATE] [\-\-ssh\-key PATH]
Create COUNT VMs for USER. VMs are linked clones from a Proxmox template
(default: server configured). Each gets a unique static IP in the user's
/26 network block.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-user USER
Username to own the VMs (required). Alphanumeric, dashes, underscores.
Determines the network block assignment.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-count COUNT
Number of VMs to create, 1\-50 (required).
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-memory MEM
Memory in MB (default: 4096).
.TP
.B \-\-cores N
CPU cores per VM (default: 2).
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-template VMID
Backend template VMID to clone from (default: server configured).
If omitted, the API server uses its configured default template.
.TP
.B \-\-ssh\-key PATH
SSH public key to inject. Local files are read by the client; otherwise the
path is interpreted on the API/backend host.
.RE
.TP
.B list [\-u USER]
List VMs with VMID, name, user, IP, and creation time.
Alias:
.BR ls .
.TP
.B get VMID
Show full details for a single VM including SSH command.
.TP
.B destroy \-\-vm VMID | \-u USER
Queue destruction of a single VM or all VMs for a user. The VM is removed from
FastVM state immediately; backend cleanup may continue asynchronously.
Networking is cleaned up automatically when a user's last VM is removed.
Alias:
.BR rm .
.TP
.B snapshot \-\-vm VMID
Convert a running VM into a reusable Proxmox template. The VM is stopped,
its SSH proxy is removed, and it becomes an immutable template that can be
cloned via
.BR "fastvm create \-t VMID" .
The VM is unregistered from active state (it is no longer a running VM).
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-vm VMID
VMID of the VM to convert (required).
.RE
.TP
.B converge \-u USER \-\-keep VMID
The key operation: keep one VM (the winner), destroy all others for that user.
Use this after parallel experiments to select the best result.
.TP
.B status
System overview: total VMs, users, per\-user network blocks and VM counts.
.TP
.B health
Verify API connectivity. Prints status and response time in milliseconds.
.TP
.B ssh VMID
Print the SSH command to connect to a VM.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FASTVM_API
Base URL of the fastvm\-api server.
Default:
.IR http://localhost:8090 .
Set this to the backend host's API address, e.g.
.IR http://atl1-pc1-025:8090 .
For Firecracker, point it at the Firecracker API host, e.g.
.IR http://atl1-pc1-027.hosts.telnyx.io:8090 .
.SH NETWORKING
Each user gets an isolated /26 block from the 192.168.0.0/16 supernet:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Block N \(-> 192.168.<N/4>.<(N%4)*64>/26
Gateway:   base + 1
VM IPs:    base + 2, base + 3, ... (up to 50 VMs)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Block assignment is deterministic (hashed from username) and persisted.
No two users can collide.
.PP
.B Internet access:
VMs reach the internet via iptables MASQUERADE on the backend host.
.PP
.B DNS:
Set to 10.255.0.100 (Telnyx internal). Resolves *.query.prod.telnyx.io,
*.query.dev.telnyx.io, and standard public domains.
.PP
.B Isolation:
Each user's VMs share a Linux bridge. Different users are on different bridges.
VMs within the same user can see each other; VMs across users cannot.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
.nf
  \fBYour machine\fR (Mac/laptop/CI)        \fBBackend Host\fR
  +------------------+               +----------------------------+
  |  fastvm (CLI)    | --HTTP--->    |  fastvm-api (Go server)    |
  |  thin HTTP client|               |    |                       |
  +------------------+               |    +-> Proxmox or Firecracker |
                                     |    +-> /var/lib/fastvm/    |
  +------------------+               |    +-> backend storage     |
  |  ssh -p <VMID>   | --TCP---->   |    +-> Linux bridge + NAT  |
  |  (SSH client)    |               |    +-> socat SSH proxy     |
  +------------------+               |         port VMID -> VM:22 |
                                     +----------------------------+
.fi
.PP
The CLI is a stateless HTTP client. All state lives on the backend host.
The API server runs co\-located with the VM backend for instant state reads and
direct backend execution (no SSH overhead).
.PP
SSH access is proxied via socat: each VM gets a listener on the backend
host's public interface at port = VMID, forwarding to the VM's internal
SSH port (22). No VPN or jump host required.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Custom template workflow (create \(-> set up \(-> snapshot \(-> diverge)
.PP
Create a base VM and set it up:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ fastvm create \-u base \-c 1
Creating 1 VM(s) for user 'base'...
VMID   NAME            IP            SSH
60000  fastvm\-base\-1  192.168.42.2  ssh \-p 60000 ubuntu@atl1\-pc1\-025

\(ok 1 VM(s) created in 12.3s

$ ssh \-p 60000 ubuntu@atl1\-pc1\-025
ubuntu@fastvm\-base\-1:~$ sudo apt install \-y nodejs npm
ubuntu@fastvm\-base\-1:~$ npm install \-g my\-app && my\-app \-\-init
ubuntu@fastvm\-base\-1:~$ exit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Snapshot the configured VM into a reusable template:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ fastvm snapshot \-\-vm 60000
Converting VM 60000 to template...
\(ok VM 60000 converted to template. Use \-t 60000 to clone from it.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Clone 5 copies from your custom template and compete:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ fastvm create \-u compete \-c 5 \-t 60000 \-\-cores 4
Creating 5 VM(s) for user 'compete'...
VMID   NAME                IP            SSH
60001  fastvm\-compete\-1   192.168.85.2  ssh \-p 60001 ubuntu@atl1\-pc1\-025
60002  fastvm\-compete\-2   192.168.85.3  ssh \-p 60002 ubuntu@atl1\-pc1\-025
60003  fastvm\-compete\-3   192.168.85.4  ssh \-p 60003 ubuntu@atl1\-pc1\-025
60004  fastvm\-compete\-4   192.168.85.5  ssh \-p 60004 ubuntu@atl1\-pc1\-025
60005  fastvm\-compete\-5   192.168.85.6  ssh \-p 60005 ubuntu@atl1\-pc1\-025

\(ok 5 VM(s) created in 22.1s
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pick the winner and clean up:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ fastvm converge \-u compete \-\-keep 60003
Converging user 'compete', keeping VMID 60003...
\(ok Converged user 'compete', kept VMID 60003

$ fastvm destroy \-u compete
Destroying all VMs for user 'compete'...
\(ok Destroyed 1 VM(s) for user 'compete'
.fi
.RE
.SS Quick diverge (skip snapshot, use stock template)
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ fastvm create \-u backend\-race \-c 3 \-\-cores 4 \-m 8192
Creating 3 VM(s) for user 'backend\-race'...
VMID   NAME                    IP            SSH
60000  fastvm\-backend\-race\-1  192.168.42.2  ssh \-p 60000 ubuntu@atl1\-pc1\-025
60001  fastvm\-backend\-race\-2  192.168.42.3  ssh \-p 60001 ubuntu@atl1\-pc1\-025
60002  fastvm\-backend\-race\-3  192.168.42.4  ssh \-p 60002 ubuntu@atl1\-pc1\-025

\(ok 3 VM(s) created in 28.4s

$ fastvm converge \-u backend\-race \-\-keep 60001
Converging user 'backend\-race', keeping VMID 60001...
\(ok Converged user 'backend\-race', kept VMID 60001
.fi
.RE
